WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.070
This is stretto.

2
00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.708
It reads ahead of your agent.

3
00:00:07.108 --> 00:00:09.868
An AI agent works in turns.

4
00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:13.728
On every turn, a large model reads the whole conversation,

5
00:00:13.728 --> 00:00:17.848
then picks one next step: answer the user, or call a tool.

6
00:00:17.848 --> 00:00:21.048
Each turn costs seconds and thousands of tokens,

7
00:00:21.048 --> 00:00:23.509
and one task can take dozens of them.

8
00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:29.929
But watch the turns closely, and many are decided before they begin.

9
00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.989
Here, the customer's record lists two orders,

10
00:00:32.989 --> 00:00:36.629
so the agent's next two turns will read those two orders.

11
00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:40.009
The tools made that choice, not the customer.

12
00:00:40.009 --> 00:00:42.332
The model just spends a turn typing it out.

13
00:00:44.832 --> 00:00:50.052
stretto sits between the agent and its tools, as an MCP proxy.

14
00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:52.712
When a result names what the agent will read next,

15
00:00:52.712 --> 00:00:54.852
stretto makes those reads at once,

16
00:00:54.852 --> 00:00:58.632
and they arrive inside the result the agent already asked for.

17
00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:02.112
The agent has them before it asks, so it skips those turns.

18
00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:05.952
There are no new tools, and the prompt doesn't change.

19
00:01:07.952 --> 00:01:10.012
It only ever reads.

20
00:01:10.012 --> 00:01:13.232
It never writes, so it can't change anything.

21
00:01:13.232 --> 00:01:16.592
When it guesses wrong, the agent gets a lookup it didn't need:

22
00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:18.858
a few extra tokens, and nothing else.

23
00:01:20.958 --> 00:01:23.498
Where do the guesses come from?

24
00:01:23.498 --> 00:01:25.578
stretto records the agent's sessions,

25
00:01:25.578 --> 00:01:30.818
and counts what it read after each call, and where each argument came from.

26
00:01:30.818 --> 00:01:34.438
That becomes a flow: a small file of counts you can read,

27
00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:36.938
review and diff, like code.

28
00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:40.286
About ten sessions teach it most of what there is to learn.

29
00:01:42.486 --> 00:01:47.526
A lookup is made when the chance that the agent uses it, before its next write,

30
00:01:47.526 --> 00:01:52.706
clears a threshold set by two costs: a turn saved, and a lookup wasted.

31
00:01:52.706 --> 00:01:55.416
That comes to about thirty percent.

32
00:01:55.416 --> 00:01:57.126
No extra model is asked.

33
00:01:57.126 --> 00:01:58.887
stretto just counts.

34
00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:04.927
Live, on τ²-bench's retail and airline tasks,

35
00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:08.987
stretto cut Claude Sonnet 5's LLM turns by twenty percent,

36
00:02:08.987 --> 00:02:14.781
Claude Haiku 4.5's by twenty-two, and GLM-5.3's by twenty-eight.

37
00:02:17.381 --> 00:02:22.021
Telling the agent to batch its calls saved only three to eight percent.

38
00:02:22.021 --> 00:02:26.305
Even with that prompt in place, stretto cut about twenty percent more.

39
00:02:28.805 --> 00:02:31.185
stretto is open source.

40
00:02:31.185 --> 00:02:35.545
Install it, run stretto init to put it in front of an MCP server,

41
00:02:35.545 --> 00:02:38.232
and it learns from your agent's own sessions.

42
00:02:40.332 --> 00:02:41.152
stretto.

43
00:02:41.152 --> 00:02:42.508
Read ahead of your agent.
